- How would you adapt for 128-bit inputs and performance considerations?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests branchless thinking and lane-wise masking, fundamental techniques in low-level libraries and SIMD operations.

## Key Concepts
- Lane-wise masking without conditionals using two's complement arithmetic
- 128-bit composition from 32-bit chunks
- Bitwise extraction and packing operations

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode C-like implementation demonstrating the core idea
uint128 blendMask128(uint128 x){
  uint32_t a3 = (x.hi >> 32) & 0xFFFFFFFF;
  uint32_t a2 = x.hi & 0xFFFFFFFF;
  uint32_t a1 = (x.lo >> 32) & 0xFFFFFFFF;
  uint32_t a0 = x.lo & 0xFFFFFFFF;
  uint64_t m3 = -(uint64_t)(a3 & 1);
  uint64_t m2 = -(uint64_t)(a2 & 1);
  uint64_t m1 = -(uint64_t)(a1 & 1);
  uint64_t m0 = -(uint64_t)(a0 & 1);
  uint128 result;
  result.hi = (m3 << 32) | m2;
  result.lo = (m1 << 32) | m0;
  return result;
}
```

## Quick Tests
- Input: 0x123456789ABCDEF0 → Output: 0x0000000000000000 (all lanes even)
- Input: 0x12345679_9ABCDEF1 → Output: 0xFFFFFFFF_00000000 (only A0 odd)

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of 128-bit data manipulation, endianness, and composing simple bit tricks to achieve a larger transformation without loops.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit value composed of two 64-bit halves
- Byte reversal within 64-bit words (endian swap)
- Combining results to form final 128-bit reversal
- No loops; relies on fixed-mask shifts

## Code Example

```javascript
uint64_t reverse64(uint64_t x){
  x = ((x & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL) << 32) | ((x & 0xFFFFFFFF00000000ULL) >> 32);
  x = ((x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16) | ((x & 0xFFFF0000FFFF0000ULL) >> 16);
  x = ((x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8)  | ((x & 0xFF00FF00FF00FF00ULL) >> 8);
  return x;
}

void endianSwap128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo, uint64_t *out_hi, uint64_t *out_lo){
  *out_hi = reverse64(lo);
  *out_lo = reverse64(hi);
}
```

