- How does this approach scale if you extend to 256-bit values?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of fast, branchless bit tricks and constant-time bit-scanning utilities common in high-performance systems.

## Key Concepts
- Isolating least-significant 1: v = n & -n
- De Bruijn-based indexing: map a 5-bit index to a trailing-zero count
- Handling edge case n == 0 (return 32)
- Static lookup table of 32 entries

## Code Example
```c
uint32_t countTrailingZeros32(uint32_t n){
  static const int debruijn[32] = {0,1,28,2,29,14,24,3,30,22,20,15,25,17,4,8,31,27,13,23,21,19,16,7,26,12,18,9,6,11,5,10};
  if(n == 0) return 32;
  uint32_t v = n & -n;
  return debruijn[(v * 0x077CB531) >> 27];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for 64-bit inputs?
- What are alternatives that avoid a static table, e.g., using binary search on bit groups?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to perform 128-bit two's complement negation and bitwise combination without loops.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit arithmetic via hi/lo halves
- Two's complement negation with carry propagation
- Bitwise AND across full 128-bit value

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 isolateLowestBit128(uint128 x){
  uint128 neg;
  neg.hi = ~x.hi;
  neg.lo = ~x.lo;
  // add 1 to lo, propagate carry
  neg.lo += 1;
  neg.hi += (neg.lo == 0);
  return (uint128){ .hi = x.hi & neg.hi, .lo = x.lo & neg.lo };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test corner cases where trailing zeros span both halves?
- How would you adapt for a wider 256-bit type?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of deeper bit-manipulation: 3D Morton ordering for spatial indexing, with no loops and tight constants.

## Key Concepts
- Morton (z-order) encoding across 3 dimensions
- bit spreading (part1by2) with masks/shifts
- avoiding branches for tight hot-paths

## Code Example
```javascript
function part1by2(n){
  let x = BigInt(n) & 0x1fffffn;
  x = (x | (x << 32n)) & 0x1f00000000ffffn;
  x = (x | (x << 16n)) & 0x1f0000ff0000ffn;
  x = (x | (x << 8n))  & 0x100f00f00f00f00fn;
  x = (x | (x << 4n))  & 0x10c30c30c30c30c3n;
  x = (x | (x << 2n))  & 0x1249249249249249n;
  return x;
}
function morton3D(x,y,z){
  return part1by2(x) | (part1by2(y) << 1n) | (part1by2(z) << 2n);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to 32-bit inputs? Trade-offs?
- Could you implement a inverse decode that recovers x,y,z from the code?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of multi-limb bit tricks and how two's complement generalizes to 128 bits without branches.

## Key Concepts

- x & (-x) principle for lsb isolation across 2 limbs
- careful carry propagation from low to high limb
- avoiding branches by using arithmetic comparisons that yield 0/1

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 isolateLowestSetBit128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t loNeg = ~x.lo + 1;
  uint64_t carry = (loNeg < ~x.lo);
  uint64_t hiNeg = ~x.hi + carry;
  uint128 r;
  r.lo = x.lo & loNeg;
  r.hi = x.hi & hiNeg;
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend this to also return the index of the isolated bit in a 0..127 range?
- What are the edge-case behaviors when x is zero or has a single 1 at the MSB?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of low-level bit hacks: packing, masking, and fixed-time transformations without control flow.

## Key Concepts
- Morton (Z-order) interleaving
- Bit spreading via staged masks
- Fixed, branchless implementation

## Code Example
```cpp
uint32_t spread16(uint16_t v){
  uint32_t x = v;
  x = (x | (x << 8)) & 0x00FF00FF;
  x = (x | (x << 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
  x = (x | (x << 2)) & 0x33333333;
  x = (x | (x << 1)) & 0x55555555;
  return x;
}
uint32_t interleave16(uint16_t a, uint16_t b){
  return spread16(a) | (spread16(b) << 1);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you generalize to interleave 32-bit values into a 64-bit result?
- What tests would you add to validate edge cases like a or b == 0xFFFF?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to derive complex bit-pattern metrics with constant-time bit tricks, not brute force.

## Key Concepts

- Identify run starts with n & ~(n << 1)
- Implement a portable popcount64 using parallel masks
- Edge-case handling for 0 and large runs

## Code Example

```c
static inline unsigned popcount64(uint64_t x) {
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  return (x * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}

static inline unsigned countRuns64(uint64_t n) {
  return popcount64(n & ~(n << 1));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt to an 128-bit value?
- What are the performance implications on large-scale bitmap indexes in practice?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to derive a compact, branch-free bit-twisting routine for combinatorial generation.

## Key Concepts

- Next permutation with fixed popcount via snoob
- Branchless 64-bit arithmetic and overflow behavior
- Edge-case handling in tests

## Code Example

```javascript
uint64_t nextWithSameBitCount(uint64_t x){
  uint64_t smallest = x & -x;
  uint64_t ripple = x + smallest;
  uint64_t ones = x ^ ripple;
  ones = (ones >> 2) / smallest;
  return ripple | ones;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt for arbitrary precision (128-bit) using two limbs?
- How would you detect when no next permutation exists efficiently?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to manipulate a 128-bit value by composing 64-bit operations, enforcing a non-loop, fixed-sequence approach for performance.

## Key Concepts
- Endianness and byte-level reversal
- Bit-twiddling for 64-bit reversal
- Combining two 64-bit halves into a 128-bit result

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
static inline uint64_t reverse64(uint64_t v) {
  v = ((v & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8) | ((v >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL);
  v = ((v & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16) | ((v >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL);
  v = (v << 32) | (v >> 32);
  return v;
}
uint128 swapEndian128(uint128 x) {
  uint128 r;
  r.lo = reverse64(x.hi);
  r.hi = reverse64(x.lo);
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt if only a single 128-bit type isn’t available?
- How would you validate correctness with representative test patterns (all zeros, all ones, alternating bits)?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to perform endianness reversal across a 128-bit value with no branches.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit layout via hi/lo; byte-level reversal; pure bit tricks.
- 64-bit byte swap implementation using shifts/masks.
- Branchless data movement across two halves.

## Code Example
```c
static inline uint64_t bswap64(uint64_t x){
  x = ((x & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL) << 32) | ((x & 0xFFFFFFFF00000000ULL) >> 32);
  x = ((x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16) | ((x & 0xFFFF0000FFFF0000ULL) >> 16);
  x = ((x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8)  | ((x & 0xFF00FF00FF00FF00ULL) >> 8);
  return x;
}
void swapEndianness128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo, uint64_t *out_hi, uint64_t *out_lo){
  *out_hi = bswap64(lo);
  *out_lo = bswap64(hi);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to a 256-bit type? (Explain any performance considerations.)
- Could you implement a generic macro to swap endianness for any multiple of 8 bytes?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests applying a simple bitwise identity (Gray code) across a non-native 128-bit representation, including cross-boundary shifts. It enforces branch-free, constant-time thinking and careful hi/lo handling.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit arithmetic using 64-bit halves
- Gray code relation: G = X ^ (X >> 1)
- Cross-boundary shifts between hi and lo

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
static inline uint128 toGray128(uint128 x){
  uint64_t hiGray = x.hi ^ (x.hi >> 1);
  uint64_t loGray = x.lo ^ ((x.lo >> 1) | (x.hi << 63));
  return (uint128){.hi = hiGray, .lo = loGray};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you verify correctness with randomized tests without branches?
- What’s the throughput difference when implemented on 128-bit wide arithmetic vs emulated two 64-bit halves?

